TY  - JOUR
AU  - Siemers, Björn Martin; Güttinger, René
TI  - Prey conspicuousness can explain apparent prey selectivity
PY  - 2006
AB  - To study prey selection, ecologists compare the abundance of food resources with the actual prey spectrum [1,2]; selectivity is inferred when some prey are markedly overrepresented in an animal's diet [3]. However, the capabilities of an animal's sensory systems and the cues provided by potential prey both constrain its foraging behavior and access to food [4–9]. Here we report evidence from a study of the greater mouse-eared bat (Myotis myotis) which supports the hypothesis that selectivity patterns can be explained in part by the specific conspicuousness of the prey to the foragers' sensory systems (‘passive prey selection’).
